  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "This is the final episode of the first season of Kukhnya, and everything is about to change. Elena comes to the apartment where Max and the Chef live early in the morning.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здрасте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это Макс?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Да, это я.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я ищу его.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дома не могу ему дозвониться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где он?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Я не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Наверное, ключи висят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Значит, он дома.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Вы хотите войти?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Проходите.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здрасте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello.' Elena delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'hello'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'good' directly with 'morning', combining 'good' with 'morning'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это Макс?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is this Max?' She wants to make sure she is talking to the right person.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Да, это я.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, it is me.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'yes' directly with 'this', combining 'yes' with 'this' and 'I'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я ищу его.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am looking for him.' Elena delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'am looking for'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дома не могу ему дозвониться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'At home I can't reach him.' Here Elena anchors the line on the word meaning 'at home' and builds the thought around 'can' and 'to get through'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где он?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is he?' Elena delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'where'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Я не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't know.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'know'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Наверное, ключи висят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Probably, the keys are hanging.' Max puts the word for 'probably' together with 'the keys' and 'to hang' in a crisp, natural spoken turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Значит, он дома.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So, he is home.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'then' directly with 'at home', combining 'then' with 'at home'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Вы хотите войти?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you want to come in?' Max turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'want', followed by 'to come in'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Проходите.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come in.' Max leads with the direct command form meaning 'come in' to make the point directly in conversation. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
